  "id": "Compound::DB00997 - Disease::DOID:1612",
  "verbalization": "Doxorubicin is implicated in the treatment of breast cancer through a complex network of molecular interactions, as evidenced by its role in causing chemical cystitis and myeloid leukaemia, both of which are treated by thiotepa, which in turn targets breast cancer. The shared lowest common ancestor of Doxorubicin and thiotepa, both classified as organic cyclic compounds and DNA-binding agents, suggests a potential mechanism of action involving interactions with nucleic acids. This is further supported by the observed relationship between doxorubicin and breast cancer, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence points to a possible role of DNA-binding properties in mediating the therapeutic effect, though the precise mechanism requires further investigation. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of DNA-binding agents in the context of cancer treatment, though the specific molecular pathways remain under study.",
